    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-strike-of-the-giants",
      "heading": "Strike of the Giants",
      "sourceAnchor": "strike-of-the-giants",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-strike-of-the-giants-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strike of the Giants provides some on-hit damage and usually a rider effect, offering some versatility for martial classes looking to do more than damage. At PB/day uses these won’t dominate your tactics, but they do give you a fun button to push a few times per day."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-strike-of-the-giants-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Because the damage is “extra damage”, it is multiplied on a critical hit. It can be very tempting to reserve Strike of the Giants for crits, but usually it’s more impactful to use it for the rider effect."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-strike-of-the-giants-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Your choice here also limits which other feat in the tree you can take. Since Strike of the Giants can’t be taken more than once, you’ll typically select your type of giant based on which of the 4th-level feats you want to take."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-strike-of-the-giants-4",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Cloud Strike"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Thunder damage is rarely\n\t\tresisted, and becoming invisible to the creature means that you can safely\n\t\twalk out of melee or just make your next attack against them with Advantage.\n\t\tThe target will struggle to attack you or target you on their following\n\t\tturn. However, the effect ends at the "
              },
              {
                "type": "emphasis",
                "text": "beginning"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " of your next turn,\n\t\tso you won’t get to attack with Advantage on your next turn."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Fire Strike"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": The most boring and\n\t\tstraightforward option, but also the biggest damage die. Unfortunately,\n\t\tresistance and immunity to fire damage are common."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Frost Strike"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": A great debuff to keep\n\t\tenemies from fleeing or running off to attack your allies, but it’s a\n\t\tConstitution save so it’s going to be unreliable, especially against the big\n\t\tmelee threats where this matters most."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Hill Strike"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": A great way to start a\n\t\tseries of follow-up attacks. However, Strength saves are often high for big\n\t\tmelee creatures, so this will be unreliable against many foes. This is a\n\t\tgreat way for melee-focused characters to handle flying enemies because\n\t\tStrike of the Giants works with thrown weapons, allowing you to knock flying\n\t\tenemies prone and force them to fall."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Stone Strike"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Force damage\n\t\tresistance/immunity are borderline non-existent, and the 10-foot movement\n\t\tcan be very helpful for breaking grapples or putting enemies somewhere\n\t\tdangerous.\n\t\t"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The phrase “pushed 10 feet from you in a straight line” is a weirdly\n\t\t\texciting development. I’ve written previously about how WotC’s refusal to\n\t\t\tsay “"
              },
              {
                "type": "emphasis",
                "text": "directly"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " away” has allowed things like\n\t\t\t"
              },
              {
                "type": "link",
                "text": "open hand monks punting",
                "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/monk/subclasses/open-hand/"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "\n\t\t\tenemies 10 feet into the air, and it appears that WotC is taking that to\n\t\t\theart and trying different wording. Unfortunately, this text is just as\n\t\t\tvague."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "\n\t\t"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "“Pushed” implies that the creature is pushed away from you, otherwise it\n\t\t\twould save “moved” or “pulled”. The text “in a straight line” implies that\n\t\t\tyou can’t change direction while moving them, so no bending the target\n\t\t\taround a corner. However, this still allows you to move targets away so\n\t\t\tlong as they end 10 feet further away from you than they started, meaning\n\t\t\tthat we’re right back to punting enemies 10 feet into the air. Even worse,\n\t\t\tthis stacks with Crusher and Open Hand Technique, so ambitious monks can\n\t\t\tnow punt enemies 25 feet into the air."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "\n\t\t"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "I do sincerely believe that the intent is to push enemies directly away\n\t\t\tfrom you in a straight, horizontal line. But that’s not what they wrote. “pushed 10 feet "
              },
              {
                "type": "emphasis",
                "text": "directly away"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " from you” was right there."
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "\n\t"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Storm Strike"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": It’s on a Constitution\n\t\tsave, which is always a gamble, but Disadvantage on attacks until your next\n\t\tturn can turn the tide of a fight."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
